  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The commemoration of the dead, with its own pause. As in the Meménto of the living, the missal's name slot N. et N. is omitted; because the edition does not print the names, the relative clause that follows describes the servants and handmaids themselves and takes no comma, which is also witness handmissal-eo's pointing. QUIESCÉNTIBUS at w024 is a participle used as a noun — those resting — and takes its dative from indúlgeas. LOCUM at w025 is the object of indúlgeas across the intervening ut, which stands late in its own clause. SIGNO FÍDEI at w011-w012 is understood of baptism — the seal received in it — and the note says so. REFRIGÉRII at w026 is the genitive singular: it stands coordinate with lucis and pacis, which leaves a locative no room at all, Lewis and Short head the noun refrigerium, ii, and Collatinus reads the genitive. Whitaker's carries THIS ENTRY with the contracted genitive only and so offers a locative — a choice made in his dictionary entry, not a rule about -ium nouns, since the same analyzer reads Evangélii as a genitive singular. The overruling is recorded in the pipeline's rulings.",
